WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.292
YURI GRINSHTEYN: As you look
to make your services more

00:00:02.292 --> 00:00:04.260
reliable and easier
to operate, logging

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:07.320
becomes increasingly important,
both during normal operation

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:08.580
and troubleshooting.

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:10.050
Today, I'm here
to show you what's

00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:12.760
new in Google Cloud Logging
and help you get started.

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:14.070
This is the Stack Doctor.

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:17.926
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:21.310 --> 00:00:23.690
We've looked at logging errors
in the previous episode,

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:26.380
but Cloud Logging has gotten
a major update since then.

00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:29.230
The Logs Viewer is entirely new,
and there are new capabilities

00:00:29.230 --> 00:00:30.130
under the hood.

00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:31.785
Let's take a look.

00:00:31.785 --> 00:00:33.660
You can access the logs
in your cloud project

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:35.460
by going to the Products
menu and selecting

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:37.185
Logging under Operations.

00:00:37.185 --> 00:00:38.810
This will bring you
to the Logs Viewer.

00:00:38.810 --> 00:00:40.740
Note that you can pin
products in the menu

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:42.750
so that they're always
at the top of the list.

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:46.320
Logging is probably one
of the ones I use most.

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:48.560
The new Logs Viewer has
three main sections.

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:50.060
The Query Builder
at the top is what

00:00:50.060 --> 00:00:51.810
you use to filter the
logs in your project

00:00:51.810 --> 00:00:53.390
to just the entries you need.

00:00:53.390 --> 00:00:56.060
The Query Results at the
bottom shows you all the log

00:00:56.060 --> 00:00:57.950
entries that match your query.

00:00:57.950 --> 00:00:59.690
The Histogram view
in the middle shows

00:00:59.690 --> 00:01:03.050
you the volume of logs that
match your query over time.

00:01:03.050 --> 00:01:05.030
This is incredibly useful
if, for example, you

00:01:05.030 --> 00:01:07.447
were looking to understand
when a particular error started

00:01:07.447 --> 00:01:08.390
happening.

00:01:08.390 --> 00:01:10.400
There are two primary
ways to create queries

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:11.480
against your logs.

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:13.640
The first one is use
the menu selections.

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:16.050
The second is to write
the query directly.

00:01:16.050 --> 00:01:18.060
Let's take a look at both.

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:20.610
First, you can use the menu
selection in the Query Builder

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:23.730
to select the resource, log
type, and log entry severity

00:01:23.730 --> 00:01:25.530
that you'd like to
include in your query.

00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:27.780
You can make your selections
as granular or as broad

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:28.620
as you like.

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:30.120
The Query Builder
will then generate

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:33.510
the string query for you
based on your selections.

00:01:33.510 --> 00:01:35.760
Your second option is to
write the query directly

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:37.642
without using the
menu selectors.

00:01:37.642 --> 00:01:39.350
This may become easier
and faster for you

00:01:39.350 --> 00:01:41.550
once you've spent some time
using the Query Builder.

00:01:41.550 --> 00:01:43.200
We've also published
a library of queries

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:44.325
you can use to get started.

00:01:44.325 --> 00:01:47.210
We'll link to that below.

00:01:47.210 --> 00:01:50.130
Note that this new Logs Viewer
is still in a preview stage.

00:01:50.130 --> 00:01:52.610
This means that lots of features
are still being developed.

00:01:52.610 --> 00:01:54.860
One of these that's only
available in the classic Logs

00:01:54.860 --> 00:01:57.600
Viewer is the ability to
save and share queries.

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:00.890
This is incredibly valuable
when working with teammates.

00:02:00.890 --> 00:02:03.490
You've now seen how to view
and query logs in your project,

00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:05.400
but how do logs
actually get there?

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:07.590
Let's take a look
under the hood.

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:10.028
The Cloud Logging API
is the external endpoint

00:02:10.028 --> 00:02:12.070
that serves as the single
entry point for sending

00:02:12.070 --> 00:02:13.840
logs to Cloud Logging.

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:15.580
No matter where logs
are coming from,

00:02:15.580 --> 00:02:17.913
whether it's from a logging
agent installed on a compute

00:02:17.913 --> 00:02:20.830
VM, your application running
in GKE or App Engine,

00:02:20.830 --> 00:02:22.570
or any of the Google
Cloud services,

00:02:22.570 --> 00:02:25.160
they're all sent
to the Logging API.

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:27.723
The API sends log
entries to a Log Router.

00:02:27.723 --> 00:02:29.140
This is the internal
plumbing that

00:02:29.140 --> 00:02:32.230
determines what happens to
log entries that it receives.

00:02:32.230 --> 00:02:34.193
The Log Router
evaluates each entry

00:02:34.193 --> 00:02:35.860
to determine whether
it meets the filter

00:02:35.860 --> 00:02:37.750
criteria for any exclusions.

00:02:37.750 --> 00:02:40.780
If an exclusion is applicable,
that entry is discarded.

00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:41.968
That's not the case?

00:02:41.968 --> 00:02:44.010
The entry is stored in
the Cloud Logging back end

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:45.385
and is then
available to BigQuery

00:02:45.385 --> 00:02:48.660
through the logs of your
or for log-based metrics.

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:50.730
The Log Router also
evaluates each entry

00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:53.250
that it receives to determine
whether it should be exported

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:54.960
to a downstream destination.

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:57.390
You can configure exports to
Cloud Storage for archiving,

00:02:57.390 --> 00:02:59.460
BigQuery for
analytics, or Pub/Sub

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:02.970
to send logs to third party
or event processing systems.

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:05.490
Note that the exclusion
and the export evaluations

00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:07.980
happen in parallel on
the same log entry.

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:11.350
That means you can both export
log entries and exclude them.

00:03:11.350 --> 00:03:14.220
This can often be used to
great effect to save costs.

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:15.567
Thanks for joining me today.

00:03:15.567 --> 00:03:17.400
We looked at the new
Logs Viewer and learned

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:18.910
how to build log queries.

00:03:18.910 --> 00:03:20.460
We also took a
look under the hood

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:22.290
and learned about how
the Log Router works

00:03:22.290 --> 00:03:24.780
to allow you to precisely
control which logs are stored,

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:26.610
discarded, or exported.

00:03:26.610 --> 00:03:27.750
That's it for this episode.

00:03:27.750 --> 00:03:29.140
Come back soon for more.

00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:30.302
This is the Stack Doctor.

00:03:30.302 --> 00:03:31.260
Stay healthy out there.

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:34.910
[MUSIC PLAYING]

